
yap:
	a small and flexible test library for c++11.

    imlobster (c) 2026
    The Unlicense

installation:
    put yap.h in your include.

usage:

	// this how i like to use it.
	// you can also split your test into multiple files:
		// void test_ABC(yap::Yap &test);

	yap::Yap test; // the test base

	test("Addition", [] YAP_begin // always wrap test code with YAP_begin and YAP_done.
		int a = 2 + 2;

		YAP_REQUIRE(a == 4); // stops this test on incident.
		YAP_CHECK(a == 5); // only notifies about incident.
		YAP_CHECK_MSG("oh no!", a == 5); // '*_MSG' versions of the macros above allow you to specify incident message.
	YAP_done);

	auto run = test.run(); // returns vector<TestResult>.

	// interpret output however you want!
	for(auto &t : run)
		for(auto &i : t.incidents)
			std::cout << t.name << ": " << i << '\n';

notes:

	compilation of tests separately from main target can be implemented in Make.
	'YAP_CHECK' and 'YAP_REQUIRE' take expressions with commas.
	'*_MSG' macros only takes string literals as messages.
	exceptions in tests are uncaught.

api:

	'struct TestResult': consists of 'std::string name' and 'std::vector<std::string> incidents'.

	'yap::Yap(name, fn)': register test (do it with rvalues pls). throws on duplicated name.
	'yap::clear()': clear tests (to reuse the test base).
	'yap::run()': runs all tests (no incidents == good).
	'yap::one(name)': runs a test. throws if not found.

